  "order": "coleoptera",
  "sex": "unsexed",
  "hallmark": "seven black spots on red wing cases",
  "reference": {
    "title": "Käferbuch. Naturgeschichte der Käfer Europas, table 46 figure 17",
    "artist": "Emil Hochdanz",
    "year": 1876,
    "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coccinella.septempunctata.-.calwer.46.17.jpg",
    "licence": "Public domain (PD-old-100-1923)"
  },
  "doc": [
    "*Coccinella septempunctata*, dorsal — the seven-spot ladybird.",
    "",
    "Traced from Emil Hochdanz's figure in Calwer's *Käferbuch* (1876) — the same",
    "book and the same hand as the stag beetle, which is why the two sit together",
    "without looking like two projects. `references/SOURCES.md` has the",
    "provenance and `references/coccinella-septempunctata.jpg` is the file.",
    "",
    "## What the drawing has to get right",
    "",
    "The spots, and their count. Seven is the animal's name: three on each wing",
    "case and one straddling the suture behind the scutellum. That last one is",
    "authored on the midline and drawn once — cut down the middle and reflected,",
    "its two halves show the join at any size worth looking at.",
    "",
    "Then the outline. A seven-spot is very nearly circular from above, the wing",
    "cases as wide across the shoulders as they are long, and the tracing reflex",
    "is to draw a beetle-shaped oval, which is a different animal. The margin",
    "points are measured off the lithograph row by row.",
    "",
    "Then the legs, which are short, thick and tucked close. At a stag beetle's",
    "proportions they turn a ladybird into a ground beetle.",
    "",
    "## Plate space",
    "",
    "Midline at x = 0, y = 0 at the front of the head and y = 1000 at the elytral",
    "apex. The fore legs and the antennae reach above y = 0, which is correct: the",
    "band measures the body and `plateViewBox` frames what is actually drawn.",
    "",
    "Drawing order is back to front, because the array is the stacking and the",
    "renderer does no sorting: legs, then the wing cases and their surface work,",
    "then the thorax, then the head. The cream pronotal patches come after the",
    "pronotum for the same reason."
  ],
